[Music]  across the country many young people are  taking  jobs to help their families make ends  meet  since the pandemic started an estimated  3 million students  have not attended virtual or in-person  classes  for some that's because they're now  working mega oliver is outside a high  school in newark new jersey with more on  teenagers  balancing school and work meg good  morning  anthony good morning we spoke with two  students one from here at arts high  school in newark new jersey  and the other in los angeles both told  us when the pandemic hit  they felt the pressure to step in and  help their families one expert told us  their stories  are not unusual several days a week  melody videl gets a ride to the mills at  jersey gardens mall where she works at  the bakery chain  cinnabon in the fall the high school  senior took the job to help her mom  financially what was your biggest fear  my biggest fear was being homeless  but trying to balance remote learning  and work is taking a toll on the 18 year  old  how overwhelming is this it's so  overwhelming overwhelmingly stressful i  find it hard to  to live sometimes you find it hard to  live sometimes yes  because of the stress maria medina is  melody's mother  she's a party entertainer and does face  and body painting  how hard has this last year been  financially for you  i watched a career that i have been  working  on for 12 years go completely dead  would you be able to make ends meet  without her helping  yes and no i mean i would i can figure  i can manage things i've been doing it  for years but her health  has been a blessing in the u.s around  17.5  million young people between 16 and 24  are employed  and many to help their families get by  seventeen-year-old joanna lopez is one  of them  why did you need this job well i wanted  to help my mom because she's  a struggling single mom the high school  senior from los angeles  juggles an exhausting load all remote  school during the day  and several days a week she works at a  fast food restaurant until midnight  her mother was laid off because of kovid  and that's when i was there i was like  okay  now it's time to actually step it up  because i'm a little brother  lopez brings in about eight hundred  dollars a month and gives 500 to her mom  for rent in other bills you're doing a  great thing for your mom  are you worried about your mom  not really i know that she's a strong  woman this has got to be overwhelming  for you  you're only 17 years old i try to keep a  positive attitude why did you want to  share your story i want other people to  see that they're not the only ones that  struggle  are you seeing more high school students  trying to bring home paycheck  absolutely young people are under  incredible pressure to contribute to the  finances in the home  elmer roald dahn is the executive  director of communities and schools of  los  angeles his organization works with  lopez and  hundreds of other students and their  families to help provide  social emotional academic and even  financial support to keep  kids in school what do you think will  happen now that some of these high  schools will start to reopen after  spring break  and you have students trying to juggle  school and jobs  well i think that it's going to put both  the schools  and the students in a very difficult  place and i think it's going to  force students to have to pick between  their education  and bringing home that income well don  says his organization tries to encourage  young people  to focus on a long-term vision for  themselves  like higher education certainly the  income that they're bringing in  right now is a huge lifeline for their  families and  you know and all jobs are respectable  but we also know that  the potential is there for them to do a  lot more  for melody just the thought of doing  more is keeping her afloat  she hopes to go to college next year you  have something to look forward to  yeah i do you're helping your family get  by  pay the rent make sure that the  electrical gets paid how does that make  you feel  it makes you feel happy makes me feel  like maybe we're not  gonna be homeless it's so scary to think  about that  so much pressure but they both have so  much potential melody tells us she wants  to study  graphic design at a community college  next year and joanna the student out in  los angeles also plans to go to college  she wants to be a music therapist  tony meg thank you very much it's a  reminder that the child  tax credit can help struggling parents  like that so maybe their kids don't have  to choose between school  and and that job to support the family  well you feel for a melody and joanna  i'm  just going to say but in the meantime to  hear melody say  sometimes i don't want to live just gave  me chills you got to hang in there  because we all know it's true it does  get better but when you're  in it it must seem extremely  overwhelming and joanna's saying you  know  no she doesn't worry about her mother  his tears are streaming down her face  i never thought of that aspect of the  the layers of covet and the  damage that it causes and the teens feel  that the degree to which it  has reverberated you know also so far  and you're looking at  kids worrying about their parents yeah  and wearing as melody says worrying  about being homeless yeah these kids  have their antenna up they're picking up  what's going on in the house  no they absorb it all right meg thank  you very much powerful story

